{"packet_id": "cc94cf649c28668c", "dimensions": {"problem_framing": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A1", "A2", "A4"], "reason": "A1 lists four obvious alternatives and separates the objective from a consumer launch. However, it introduces constraints that appear nowhere in the case or sources: a €20,000 test budget, a 12-week timeline, and a B2B portfolio of 'shipping, aircraft, military, marine'. Only military appears, and only as a company claim in acr-mr-military. An invented number caps the dimension at 1. A1 also misses a premise visible in sources returned at A2 and A4. acr-gcn-three shows ACR's GyPSI 406 is already an FCC-approved PLB. That fact bears on whether a 'consumer proposition' is new entry or differentiation against McMurdo and Microwave Monolithics."}, "causal_mechanism": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9"], "reason": "T1 (A9) names who changes behaviour (remote users), why (beacon works without cellular coverage, NOAA/RTCM certification) and how value is captured (direct premium sales). The capture link rests on an unsupported 'established safety reputation' among consumers. The 'switch from phone, radio' step also sits uneasily with acr-fr-purpose, which frames the PLB as a last resort used after those means are exhausted. A full chain is not established."}, "distinct_theories": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "T1 and T2 share the same channel (direct consumer sales), the same capture mechanism (premium price, B2B reputation) and the same prediction structure. They differ only in the motivational framing: life-saving versus convenience. They also differ by a nominal 15% versus 20% premium and a 5% versus 8% conversion rate. They are effectively rewordings of one idea, not rivals implying different products, customers or channels."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The predictions are observable: survey premium intent, pilot conversion and competitor share. The thresholds are unexplained assumptions, not results. The assumptions are partly vague, e.g. 'Price sensitivity is low'. The competitor-share prediction depends on an asserted reputation. Neither theory would clearly be in trouble if its prediction failed rather than its rival's."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15"], "reason": "Each assessment notes what the source does not show, but sources are misclassified. Verified regulatory and press observations are labelled 'independent_study' throughout A11-A15. A11 marks acr-fr-purpose as supporting T1, although regulatory intended-use text says nothing about adoption or premium willingness to pay. The company claims are never assessed. No assessment addresses date, place or customer fit, e.g. the Alaska-only rescue count in acr-gcn-alaska. Competitive implications of acr-gcn-three are ignored."}, "seeking_challenge": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A13"], "reason": "There was a single challenge search against T1 (A13). The trajectory never reports what that search found or that it found nothing against T1. It also never notes the 'after all other means... exhausted' last-resort framing in acr-fr-purpose, which cuts against the premium-switching story. No challenge against T2 was attempted."}, "experiment_design": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A16"], "reason": "The A16 predictions for T1 and T2 are identical: both groups at ≥60% intent with 20% and 15% premiums. The two decision-rule branches have identical conditions but different labels, so no outcome can favour one theory over the other. The 'primary motivation' measure could discriminate but is not tied to the decision rule. The cost and sample size are proposed assumptions. The test is feasible, but it is non-discriminating."}, "revision": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A18"], "reason": "SYN-D is a simulated warehouse result that measures nothing about wearers or buyers. A18 correctly identifies it as irrelevant and retains T1, which is a proportionate, evidence-linked retention. It does not trace implications for the recommendation, though none were needed."}, "recommendation": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A17", "A19"], "reason": "A19 commits to a next action and gives reversal conditions, though the 40-60% intent band is left without a decision. The 'T1: likely' belief is unsupported, since no source evidences consumer demand or willingness to pay. The claim that the test 'will resolve' uncertainty about market size is unsupported, because a 300-person intent survey cannot size a market. The favoured theory is not separable from T2 by the proposed test."}}, "red_flags": [{"kind": "non_discriminating_test", "event_ids": ["A16", "A19"], "reason": "The predictions and decision-rule conditions for T1 and T2 are identical, so the proposed survey cannot separate the rivals, yet the recommendation relies on it."}, {"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A9", "A10", "A17", "A19"], "reason": "ACR's 'established safety reputation' among consumers is asserted without a source. 'T1: likely' is asserted with no demand evidence."}, {"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A11"], "reason": "A regulatory intended-use statement is counted as support for consumer adoption at a premium. It also implies a last-resort role that tensions with T1's 'switch from phone, radio' mechanism."}, {"kind": "invented_observation", "event_ids": ["A1"], "reason": "The €20,000 / 12-week constraints and the 'shipping, aircraft' B2B portfolio are presented as case constraints without any source."}, {"kind": "other", "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15"], "reason": "Verified observations are mislabelled as 'independent_study', which misrepresents the evidence class."}], "suitable_for_training": false, "screen_reason": "The episode is complete, but the central experiment (A16) is non-discriminating, with identical predictions and decision branches for both theories. The favoured belief is also unsupported, and framing constraints are invented. This critical error alone rejects the trajectory."}
